In April the desk began taking reports about bookings landing on the wrong day. On 2 July it
merged every one of them into [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) and marked the ticket **known**: one
parser bug, one fix queued. The export holds 427 rows; 15 are the same report received twice from
overlapping export pages, so the desk reads **412 reports**.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is two problems merged as one. One cause owns the ticket. The other was merged into it by
mistake and had no ticket of its own — the desk only sees that when it reads each report's cause,
not its subject.
</Callout>

## What was really merged

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table above is the desk's own `CauseTable`, run over the export at render time: 412 reports,
**2 causes**. `parser` is the cause most reports share, so it owns #4471. `hint` is marked *not
this ticket — no ticket of its own*. Both are problems this ticket holds; both now have their own
ticket in this repo.

## parser — 379 reports

The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes dates day first, so
their dates come out flipped: a "7 April" reminder goes out on 4 July.

- All 11 tenants, all three channels: app 204 · email 150 · form 25. The 25 form reports run
  booking-v2, whose DD/MM/YYYY hint agrees with how these tenants read — the hint does not flip
  them; the parser does.
- Seen 2026-04-01 → 2026-10-04, still arriving when the export was cut.
- Intake tagged 356 of these `parser` and 23 `hint`, guessed from the reporter's browser locale.
  The tag is a guess; the cause depends on the tenant, which intake never looks up.
- Five reports (R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344, R-41620) read as a one-day move — a timezone
  edge, like a client in Lisbon getting Tuesday while Toronto gets Monday. The desk read them
  differently but files them under `parser` by tenant, counted here and footnoted, per
  desk-notes.
- Ticket: [#1 · parser — 379 reports](https://github.com/Zenit-os/support-kb-4471/issues/1)

## hint — 33 reports

booking-v3 prints the hint MM/DD/YYYY under its date field. The three tenants running it —
alder.physio (de-DE), copper.studio (es-ES), pale.harbour (pt-PT) — all write dates day first, so
they type what the hint shows, month first, and the form reads their input day first: booked 6
August, invite out on 8 June.

- All 33 reports came through the form channel. The same three tenants' 63 app and email reports
  are the parser problem, not this one — the hint only bites where it is printed.
- Seen 2026-04-17 → 2026-09-22 (alder.physio 12 · copper.studio 11 · pale.harbour 10).
- Intake tagged 15 of these `parser`. Again the browser locale, not the tenant, decided.
- This is a different bug from `parser`: here the tenant's input matches the form's own hint, and
  the form disagrees with itself.
- Ticket: [#2 · hint — 33 reports](https://github.com/Zenit-os/support-kb-4471/issues/2)

## Why they merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) made a duplicate a matching subject line.
Both problems write the same subjects — "Invite came on the wrong day", "Month and day swapped?"
— so subject matching could not tell them apart, and nobody read the cause. The
[triage explorer](/kb/triage/dedupe) runs the desk's cause test on exactly those pairs: same
subject, different causes, keep apart.

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The desk kept working through all of it: reports arrived every week from 2026-03-30 until the
export was cut on 2026-10-04, and were merged into #4471 as they came.

379 + 33 = 412: every unique report in the export is covered by exactly one of the two tickets.

## Where #4471 ended

On 5 October the desk closed [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) with an engineering update:

- **parser — FIXED**: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. The parser now reads dates the
  way EU locales write them. The 379 reports above are why the fix exists.
- **hint — WON'T FIX**: the booking-v3 form was retired. The 33 reports above end with the
  product, not with a code fix — the desk closed them anyway, with the counts on record.

Every one of the 412 reports is now accounted for: fixed, or retired with the form that caused
it.

